  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following represents the correct order of increasing first ionization enthalpy for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ba</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ar</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ba</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ar</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ba</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ar</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ba</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ar</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ba</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Ar</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ba</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ar</mi></math></p><p><br />Ba and&nbsp;Ca belong to group&nbsp;IIA ( alkaline earth metals).<br /><br />Se and S belong to group Vl A (Chalcogens) and as we go down the group ionization enthalpy decreases with increasing atomic size. So,<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ba</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and&#160;</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Se</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math><br />Ar has stable completely filled electronic configuration and, therefore,&nbsp;has the highest ionization enthalpy.Hence,&nbsp;the correct order is<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable equalcolumns=\"true\" equalrows=\"true\"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi>Ba</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>&#60;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>Ca</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>&#60;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>Se</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>&#60;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>&#60;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>Ar</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>in</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>kJmol</mi><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>503</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mn>590</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mn>941</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mn>999</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mn>1520</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>"
  }
}